---
import { getEntry } from 'astro:content';
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import ToolWorkbench from '../../components/ToolWorkbench.astro';
import ExplainerBand from '../../components/ExplainerBand.astro';
import { parseHtml } from '../../lib/accname/adapters/parse5';
import { analyze, DEFAULT_HTML } from '../../lib/accname/model';
import { analysisView, optionsView, verdictView } from '../../lib/accname/views';
import { renderToString } from '../../lib/view/h';
import '../../styles/accessible-name.css';

const tool = await getEntry('tools', 'accessible-name');
if (!tool) throw new Error('Missing tools/accessible-name.md');
const result = analyze(DEFAULT_HTML, 0, parseHtml);
if (!result.ok) throw new Error(result.error.message);
---

<ToolLayout
  tool={tool}
  title="Accessible Name Calculator: what screen readers announce"
  heading="Which accessible name wins?"
  lead="Accessible Name Wars: paste HTML and follow the label the browser chooses."
  app="accessible-name"
>
  <ToolWorkbench inputLabelledby="name-input-label">
    <Fragment slot="input">
      <p class="notice" data-name-notice role="status" hidden></p>
      <div class="field">
        <label class="label" id="name-input-label" for="name-html">
          Your HTML
        </label>
        <textarea
          class="input name-html"
          id="name-html"
          rows="5"
          spellcheck="false"
          autocapitalize="off"
          autocomplete="off"
          aria-describedby="name-help"
          data-name-html
        >{DEFAULT_HTML}</textarea>
        <p class="input-help" id="name-help">
          Up to 4 KB. Your markup is parsed, never rendered. Scripts and resources in it do not run or load.
          Only Copy link puts HTML in the URL; links are not encrypted.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="field js-only" data-name-select hidden={result.value.elements.length < 2}>
        <label class="label" for="name-element">
          Element to check
        </label>
        <select
          class="input select"
          id="name-element"
          data-name-element
          set:html={optionsView(result.value).map(renderToString).join('')}
        />
      </div>
      <div class="duel-actions js-only">
        <button class="button" type="button" data-name-copy>
          Copy link
        </button>
        <p class="status" data-name-copy-status role="status"></p>
      </div>
      <div class="field" data-name-link-wrap hidden>
        <label class="label" for="name-link">
          Link to this calculation
        </label>
        <input class="input" id="name-link" type="text" readonly data-name-link />
      </div>
      <p class="visually-hidden" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true" data-name-live></p>
      <div class="flow" data-name-answer set:html={verdictView(result.value).map(renderToString).join('')} />
    </Fragment>
    <div
      slot="output"
      class="flow"
      data-name-output
      set:html={analysisView(result.value).map(renderToString).join('')}
    />
  </ToolWorkbench>
  <ExplainerBand slot="explainer" labelledby="name-explainer">
    <div class="prose flow">
      <h2 id="name-explainer">A label, not everything on screen</h2>
      <p class="prose">
        An accessible name identifies an element; a description adds context. A screen reader can combine the
        name with its role and state. The wording depends on the reader and platform, not just the HTML. See{' '}
        <a href="https://w3c.github.io/accname/#intro">the accessible name specification</a>.
      </p>
      <h3>Follow the order, then the exceptions</h3>
      <p class="prose">
        For an element that permits naming, <code>aria-labelledby</code> is checked before
        <code>aria-label</code>, followed by native labels such as <code>label</code>, <code>alt</code>,
        <code>legend</code>, and <code>caption</code>. Roles such as button and link can then use their
        content. A <code>title</code> can be a fallback; supported text fields can finally use a placeholder.
        An empty naming source can fall through, except where the element-specific rules say otherwise. Read{' '}
        <a href="https://w3c.github.io/accname/#computation-steps">the computation steps</a> and
        <a href="https://w3c.github.io/html-aam/#accname-computation">HTML's element-specific rules</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="prose">
        A hidden element is normally excluded, but a directly referenced hidden label can contribute its
        hidden subtree. A control embedded in another label contributes its value. A generic container cannot
        simply be given a name with ARIA. These are algorithm rules, not a universal attribute ranking. See{' '}
        <a href="https://w3c.github.io/accname/#comp_hidden_not_referenced">hidden labels</a>,
        <a href="https://w3c.github.io/accname/#comp_embedded_control">embedded controls</a>, and
        <a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/wai-aria-1.2/#namefromprohibited">roles that prohibit naming</a>.
      </p>
      <h3>Keep what people see in what they hear</h3>
      <p class="prose">
        A competing ARIA label can replace a perfectly useful visible label. Voice-control users need the
        visible wording in the accessible name. Case and ordinary punctuation do not have to match. Symbolic
        characters and surrounding visual context need human review. See{' '}
        <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/label-in-name.html">WCAG 2.5.3: Label in Name</a>
        .
      </p>
      <h3>Names and descriptions have different rules</h3>
      <p class="prose">
        Descriptions check <code>aria-describedby</code>, then <code>aria-description</code>, unused native
        description sources, and finally an unused <code>title</code>. The first applicable description source
        wins even if it is empty. A <code>figcaption</code> does not automatically name its
        <code>figure</code>; reference it explicitly when appropriate. See{' '}
        <a href="https://w3c.github.io/accname/#mapping_additional_nd_description">description precedence</a>
        and{' '}
        <a href="https://w3c.github.io/html-aam/#figure-element-accessible-name-computation">figure naming</a>
        .
      </p>
      <h3>What pasted HTML cannot tell us</h3>
      <p class="prose">
        This tool reads attributes and text, not the rendered page. It recognizes <code>hidden</code>,
        <code>aria-hidden="true"</code>, and inline hiding styles. It cannot know class-based styling,
        generated text, layout-dependent whitespace, shadow-tree changes, or values changed by scripts.
        Browser-generated button labels are shown in English and can be localized by a real browser. Always
        verify the final page with your browser and assistive technology. See{' '}
        <a href="https://w3c.github.io/accname/#comp_name_from_content">rendered and generated content</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </ExplainerBand>
</ToolLayout>

<script>
  import '../../scripts/accessible-name';
</script>
